# 13. Benefits-realization model

Before investment approval, freeze a benefits register containing:

- baseline measure;
- owner;
- calculation method;
- timing;
- confidence rating;
- one-time versus recurring classification;
- cash versus noncash classification;
- dependencies;
- evidence required; and
- treatment in the NPV.

## Benefit categories

### Hard cash benefits

- eliminated payroll;
- eliminated agency labor;
- reduced overtime;
- reduced facility expansion;
- reduced customer credits and returns;
- reduced recruitment and training cost.

### Capacity benefits

- additional units or orders processed;
- deferred building expansion;
- peak capacity improvement.

Capacity is not a financial benefit until it is used for profitable demand.

### Quality benefits

- fewer errors;
- fewer returns;
- fewer credits;
- fewer customer escalations;
- reduced rework.

### Strategic benefits

- labor-market resilience;
- improved scalability;
- reduced concentration risk;
- enhanced service capability.

Strategic benefits should be reported separately unless they can be converted into measurable cash value.

## Validation thresholds

The program should proceed to the next wave only when:

- actual hard cash benefits reach at least **90% of the risk-adjusted approved benefits** by month six after stabilization;
- actual service and quality benefits meet the approved case;
- no more than 10% of claimed savings remain dependent on unapproved assumptions;
- the site has passed the independent finance review; and
- internal audit has confirmed that savings are not merely labor-hour reductions or accounting reallocations.

The 90% threshold applies to the **risk-adjusted approved case**, not the unadjusted vendor claim.

Benefits must be audited at six, 12 and 18 months after go-live.

